Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: “Material Adverse Effect” means any event, change, effect, or circumstance that, individually or in the aggregate, has or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on (i) the business, financial condition, or results of operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole, or (ii) the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement. However, with respect to clause (i) only, the following shall not be deemed to constitute a Material Adverse Effect: (a) changes in global economic conditions; (b) changes in financial markets; (c) changes in laws or regulations; (d) acts of war or terrorism; (e) natural disasters or pandemics; (f) the announcement or pendency of this Agreement; (g) any action taken with the consent of the Parent. It is understood that the effects described in clauses (a) through (g) will be considered to the extent they have a disproportionate effect on the Company compared to other companies in the same industry.\nAnswer:
Choices:

